# The 差异 tab's three buttons, and what it took to make one of them undo a write (2026-10-04)

The browser shipped a 差异 panel with Before and After panes and two buttons, 接受变更 and
回滚变更. On the host a user actually runs, clicking either one did nothing at all, and after
this batch found the reason, it turned out a second thing was also untrue: a refused rollback
was reported to the browser as a resolution. This measures both, records what the undo point
now guarantees, and states what it still does not.

Headline figures: 18 engine tests, 11 host tests against the built binary, 46 reducer tests,
6 browser cases on two viewports, and 16 source mutations of which all 16 are caught. Three
mutations survived their first run, and each survivor is a real hole in a test that looked
fine; they are in section 4 rather than left out.

== 0. Two claims that did not hold before this batch

`TODO.md` said the engine emits `diff_resolved` only after a successful adapter call, and that
the Diff boundary was covered by a real workspace. Neither described the shipped code.

First, nothing outside a test module implemented the seam:

    $ git grep -n 'DiffAdapter for' e75ee6c7 -- '*.rs'
    e75ee6c7:crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs:3641:    impl DiffAdapter for FixtureDiff {
    e75ee6c7:crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/tests/m1_flow.rs:23:impl DiffAdapter for DiffLog {
    e75ee6c7:crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/tests/workspace_diff_flow.rs:15:impl DiffAdapter for FileDiff {

Three implementations, all inside `mod tests` or a `tests/` file. The host's own engine
builder never called `with_diff_adapter`, so every 接受/回滚 click reached
"未配置 Diff adapter", which the browser received as `Error{code: "diff_failed"}`. That is why
`workspace_diff_flow.rs` and `m1_flow.rs` could stay green for weeks: both installed their own
fake, so the missing production implementation was never on the path under test.

Second, `resolve_diff` pushed `DiffResolved` unconditionally, next to the error:

    // before
    if let Err(message) = result {
        events.push(ServerMessage::Error { code: "diff_failed".into(), message });
    }
    events.push(ServerMessage::DiffResolved { proposal_id, action, sequence });

`diff_resolved` is what tells the browser to drop the preview, so a refusal arrived as "the
preview is gone, nothing more to see" while the file had not moved. The row claiming the
success-only behaviour was therefore not just incomplete, it was the opposite of the code.
The fix is one binding and one guard, and `m1_flow.rs`'s third assertion moved from
`diff_resolved` to `Audit{action: "accept_diff", outcome: "rejected"}` accordingly.

== 1. The first thing the browser found was not the undo point

The new spec drove write → preview → rollback against the built `chaos-web` binary and failed
all six cases. The cause was not the engine: `previewDiff`, `acceptDiff` and `rollbackDiff` in
`apps/chaos-ui/src/main.tsx` sent their frames without `session_id`, which the protocol
requires, so the host answered `invalid_message` and the buttons had been dead in a different
way than the missing adapter made them. `send` was then typed as `ClientMessage` rather than
a loose object, which is what keeps that from happening again:

    $ cd apps/chaos-ui && npx tsc --noEmit
    src/main.tsx(619,10): error TS2345: Argument of type '{ type: "rollback_diff"; client_msg_id: `${string}-${string}-${string}-${string}-${string}`; proposal_id: string; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'ClientMessage'.
      Property 'session_id' is missing in type '{ type: "rollback_diff"; client_msg_id: `${string}-${string}-${string}-${string}-${string}`; proposal_id: string; }' but required in type '{ type: "rollback_diff"; client_msg_id: string; session_id: string; proposal_id: string; }'.

That is what the message says today, produced by deleting `session_id` from the fixed call
once the fix was in, and the file was restored byte-identically afterwards. Doing it to all
three diff calls at once answers a question the fix otherwise leaves open -- whether the
loose typing had been hiding the same omission elsewhere in the same file:

    $ cd apps/chaos-ui && npx tsc --noEmit 2>&1 | grep -c "error TS"
    3
    $ grep -c "send({" src/main.tsx
    23

Three errors in a file with 23 `send({...})` call sites, one per diff call, so the other 20
frames were already complete and the omission was confined to the 差异 panel. The browser
case catches the same omission at run time, which matters because the dev server transpiles
without type-checking:

    KILLED   t03-rollback-sends-no-session: exit=1 failing=4
        1 [desktop-chromium] › e2e/diff-undo.pw.ts:50:1 › a landed write offers its own undo
          point and 回滚变更 puts the file back (6.0s)
        3 [desktop-chromium] › e2e/diff-undo.pw.ts:110:1 › the host refuses to roll back over
          an edit made outside the browser, and says why (6.1s)
        4 [mobile-chromium] › e2e/diff-undo.pw.ts:50:1 › a landed write offers its own undo
          point and 回滚变更 puts the file back (6.1s)
        6 [mobile-chromium] › e2e/diff-undo.pw.ts:110:1 › the host refuses to roll back over
          an edit made outside the browser, and says why (6.1s)

Four of six, not six: the 接受变更 case does not click 回滚变更, so dropping the field from
one call cannot touch it. A mutant that only some tests can see is the expected shape; the two
that pass are the record of which case does not depend on that call.

== 2. What the undo point is, in the terms the tests use

`chaos_engine::WorkspaceDiffAdapter` is a `DiffAdapter` over the workspace the process already
owns. The rules the tests pin down:

- A write records what it replaced once its bytes have landed. Before those bytes are read
  during the approved-write arm, because nothing can recover them afterwards; `None` means no
  undo point rather than a half-known one.
- `接受` writes nothing. The file is already what the user approved; accepting says it stays,
  which also takes the undo away.
- `回滚` puts the recorded bytes back, or removes the path when there was nothing there, and
  refuses with `"{path} 在写入之后又被改过，回滚会覆盖那次修改"` when the file no longer holds
  what this proposal wrote. A later edit by the user or by `git` is not this proposal's to
  erase.
- A refusal consumes nothing, so the same proposal can be rolled back once the file matches
  again.
- Both sides must be text and at most `WORKSPACE_READ_LIMIT` (1 MiB). A binary target is
  refused before the write, with `file_not_text`, rather than written and then left
  un-displayable.
- `DIFF_PROPOSAL_LIMIT` is 32. The oldest proposal is evicted past that, and an evicted
  proposal refuses with `提案不存在或已处理` instead of rolling anything back.
- Every path goes through the same root confinement the write path uses, resolved when the
  change is recorded and again on each of `read_raw`, `write_raw` and `remove`.

The host installs it in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/src/main.rs`, on the one arm of the
engine builder that has a workspace to write back into. `tests/host_startup.rs` reaches that
builder through the built binary, using the `start_host` helper the file already had.
`with_workspace_diff_adapter` refuses to be installed twice and refuses a host with no
workspace, so an engine cannot end up with two adapters or an adapter with nothing to write
to.

An earlier draft of this batch moved the whole engine builder out of `main.rs` into a `host`
module so a test could call it directly. Nothing but `main.rs` ended up calling it, and moving
it out left `main.rs` holding no Chinese at all, which the localization guard reports as a
path that exists and lost its Chinese (that class cannot be allow-listed). The extraction is
reverted and the seam is installed where the builder already lives.

The adapter keeps its undo log behind one `Mutex` with exactly one locking method, `fn log`,
and the panic-site census counts that method: `scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv` moves
`chaos-engine` from `5 8 0 1` to `5 9 0 1`, one `expect` added and no `unwrap`. Three separate
`.lock().unwrap()` sites would have been three additions to a count the gate refuses to let
grow, and one named method says what a poisoned lock actually means here -- an undo point was
being added or spent when something panicked, so the log may hold half of one.

