The three new host tests spawn `env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_chaos-web")` with `CHAOS_WORKSPACE_ROOT`
pointed at a temp directory and speak WebSocket to it, so they exercise the binary a user
runs rather than an engine assembled in the test. They are what make the m13 mutation below
mean something.

== 4. Mutation proof, including the three that first survived

Method, once for all sixteen: the source is copied aside, one seam is broken by an exact
replacement, the suite that should notice is run, and the file is restored with a plain copy
and verified with `cmp`. The driver aborts before touching anything if the baseline is not
green, and restores even when it fails halfway.

Killed on the first run (failing tests are named once each, from the driver's own log):

    m01-rollback-guard-inverted        6 failing, from `!=` to `==` in the changed-file guard
    m02-rollback-guard-blind           2 failing, the read replaced by the recorded bytes
    m03-binary-refusal-never-fires     1 failing, `a_binary_target_refuses_the_write...`
    m05-rollback-restores-after        3 failing, the restore writes the new side back
    m06-created-rollback-truncates     1 failing, `rollback_of_a_created_file_takes_the_path_away`
    m07-accept-keeps-undo              1 failing, `accept_keeps_the_bytes_and_spends_the_undo`
    m08-preview-spends-undo            6 failing, `preview` reading with `take` not `peek`
    m09-refusal-reports-success        1 failing, `let resolved = true`
    m10-write-forgets-prior-bytes      5 failing, `prior = Some(None)`
    m13-host-installs-no-seam          3 failing, the three host_startup tests named in § 3

and in the UI:

    KILLED   t01-refusal-branch-never-taken: exit=1 failing=2
        keeps the preview visible when the host refuses to undo it 6ms
        src/session.test.ts > diff review projection > keeps the preview visible when the host
          refuses to undo it
    KILLED   t02-refusal-clears-the-preview: exit=1 failing=2
        keeps the preview visible when the host refuses to undo it 6ms
        src/session.test.ts > diff review projection > keeps the preview visible when the host
          refuses to undo it

Two names for one case: vitest reports a failing test both in its progress line and in its
summary, and the driver lists every line it recognised. The third reducer case, which asserts
that an unrelated error code is not treated as a diff refusal, stays green under both mutants,
which is the half of the branch that must not change.

Three survivors, all of them findings about the tests rather than about the code:

**m04, `DIFF_PROPOSAL_LIMIT` raised from 32 to 4096, survived.** The eviction test loops
`0..=DIFF_PROPOSAL_LIMIT`, so raising the constant moves the goalposts with it: eviction still
happens, just later, and the test is written against the same number it was supposed to check.
The value is a product decision, not an implementation detail, so the test now states it:

    assert_eq!(DIFF_PROPOSAL_LIMIT, 32);

**m12, the symlink check removed from `WorkspaceAdapter::target`, survived.** The symlink test
was refusing for the wrong reason. The file behind the link held bytes different from what the
proposal recorded, so the changed-file guard fired first and confinement never got a turn. The
test now parks a file holding exactly the bytes the proposal recorded as its new side, which
leaves root confinement as the only thing between the restore and a file outside the
workspace. It kills m12, and so does the pre-existing
`approved_write_rejects_dangling_symlink_escape`.

**m11, the parent-directory check removed from the same function, survived the whole
package.** This one was a real gap: every test that reaches `target()` with a symlink puts
the symlink at the final path component, where the final-component check catches it --
`workspace_symlink_escape_is_rejected` uses `link.txt`,
`approved_write_rejects_dangling_symlink_escape` uses `dangling.txt`, and the batch's own
`rollback_cannot_be_redirected_by_a_symlink_swapped_in_after_the_write` uses `note.txt`. The
fourth symlink test, `workspace_and_attachment_stagers_reject_staging_symlink_escape`, does
put a symlinked directory in front of a path, but the workspace is refused while it is being
opened, so `target()` never runs. A symlinked directory carrying a write for a path that
does not exist yet is the one shape where only the parent check can fire, because there is
no final component to inspect. Nothing tested that, so nothing noticed its check being
deleted. `an_approved_write_through_a_symlinked_directory_stays_inside_the_workspace` now
does:

    KILLED   m11-target-drops-parent-confinement: exit=101 failing=1
        an_approved_write_through_a_symlinked_directory_stays_inside_the_workspace

One apparent kill during those re-runs was not a kill, and the driver could not tell. The
second pass used the whole-package command, to make sure no other target in `chaos-engine`
could see these mutants either, and reported:

    KILLED   m11-target-drops-parent-confinement: exit=101 failing=1
        remote::client::tests::a_transport_that_comes_up_later_is_waited_for

That test has nothing to do with path confinement. It is a deadline test: it binds port 0,
releases it, and expects a peer that rebinds it after 250 ms to need at least one retry, so a
concurrent test that grabs the same port first makes it fail. It failed on an unrelated mutant
run, and the driver, which classifies any named failing test as a kill, called it a survivor
turned victim. The pass was therefore re-run with the one mutant isolated, which is the m11
result quoted above; the same test also appeared alongside the real one in m04's second pass
(`only_the_newest_writes_stay_undoable` plus the same `remote::client` case), where the real
killer was present and the verdict happened to be right anyway. The flake is filed in
`docs/ci-test-debt.md` rather than left as an unexplained line in a log.

One mutant had to be run twice because the file it lives in moved. m13 breaks the host's
installation of the seam; the first pass patched the engine builder inside a `host` module the
batch had extracted out of `main.rs`, and that extraction was reverted afterwards for the
reason section 2 gives, which left the mutant's anchor pointing at a file no longer in the
tree. Re-pointed at `crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/src/main.rs` and run again, with the baseline
for that command green first (`host_startup` 11 passed, `m1_flow` 4 passed):

    KILLED   m13-host-installs-no-seam: exit=101 failing=3
        the_built_host_offers_an_undo_point_for_a_write_that_landed
        the_built_host_keeps_an_accepted_write_and_spends_the_undo_point
        the_built_host_refuses_to_roll_back_over_an_edit_it_did_not_make

The same three names as the first pass, so the sixteen-killed figure counts one run per mutant
against the files exactly as they are committed. The package counts in section 3 were
re-measured after the revert too and did not move.

Counting the survivors as the cost they represent: two of the three were tests that would
have passed with the protection removed, and one was a constant no test could move.

